<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Perpignan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Perpignan is a city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, the historic capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a> and of French Catalonia. It became the seat of the counts of Roussillon in the 12th century and from 1276 to 1344 the mainland capital of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a>, whose Palace of the Kings of Majorca still stands. It passed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to France with Roussillon by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Treaty of the Pyrenees</a> in 1659.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/perpignan.jpg" alt="Perpignan" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jorge Franganillo · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Perpignan_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Perpignan lies in the middle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a> plain at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, 13 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast, and is the southernmost city of metropolitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The Têt, the largest river of Roussillon, and its tributary the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basse-santa-su">Basse</a> cross it. It is the prefecture of the Pyrénées-Orientales department, with 119,656 inhabitants in 2021. Its name appears in 927 as Perpinianum and probably derives from the Roman name Perpennius.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Settlement in the area goes back to Roman times, but the medieval town seems to have been founded around the beginning of the tenth century, and it soon became the capital of the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a>. In 1172 Count Girard II bequeathed his lands to the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. The town gained the institutions of a partly self-governing commune in 1197. When James I the Conqueror founded the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> in 1276, Perpignan became the capital of its mainland territories. The decades that followed are regarded as the city's golden age, with cloth, leatherwork and goldsmithery. King Philippe III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> died there in 1285 on his return from a failed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the Crown of Aragon.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iv-of-aragon">Peter IV of Aragon</a> annexed the kingdom in 1344, and a few years later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> took about half the population. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI of France</a> occupied the city in 1463, and a rising against French rule in 1473 was put down after a long siege. Charles VIII restored it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a> in 1493. The French besieged and captured it again in September 1642, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ceded it formally 17 years later in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Treaty of the Pyrenees</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Palace of the Kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a>, of the thirteenth century, stands on the citadel behind ramparts that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s engineer Vauban updated in the seventeenth century. The town walls were razed in 1904, but the Castillet, the main gate, a fourteenth-century fortress later used as a prison, survives. Work on the cathedral began in 1324 and finished in 1509. In 1963 the painter Salvador Dalí declared the railway station the centre of the universe. The city hosts the Visa pour l'Image festival of photojournalism each September, and many street signs are in both French and Catalan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Perpignan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Appeals went to the co-princes' own courts, the episcopal tribunal at La Seu d'Urgell and a French tribunal at <b>Perpignan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/andorra">Andorra · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts ruled from <b>Perpignan</b>, which grew into a prosperous town on the route between Catalonia and Languedoc.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-roussillon">County of Roussillon</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom was a Mediterranean trading state, with Palma and <b>Perpignan</b> as royal seats, merchant fleets reaching North Africa and a famed school of cartographers who made the Catalan Atlas.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-majorca">Kingdom of Majorca</a></li><li><blockquote>Known only from Greek and Roman writers such as Avienus, Pomponius Mela and Pliny, the Sordones held the coastal plain around Ruscino (Château-Roussillon, near <b>Perpignan</b>).</blockquote><a href="/state/sordones">Sordones</a></li></ul></section>
